*Abstract.* When a robot revisits a remembered object, an occluder can hide both the object and the evidence of its removal. We audit memory updates by rendering the same location with the target retained and removed. Three two-image verifiers received 1,280 blinded queries from 16 fresh layouts in Isaac Sim and MuJoCo. After a 6 cm camera shift and 3° yaw, dense registration increased correct clear removals from 67/80 to 76/80 in Isaac and from 63/80 to 78/80 in MuJoCo. It also introduced two false removals on MuJoCo shelves. On the same 834 queries admitted by dense registration, a zero-shot vision-language model gained 20 correct removal decisions and made 49 additional false removals relative to the geometric decision rule. At the same 88 fully hidden positions, MuJoCo produced identical inputs across opposite target states and Isaac produced unequal images. Homography, dense geometry and the registered language model committed on zero, one and four of those 88 exact pairs. Each such pair yielded one correct and one false removal. In eight new MuJoCo corridors with a closed door, the registered system called both states removed in zero oblique and seven frontal pairs. After furnishing the same corridors and rebuilding the doorway, registration stopped all eight frontal pairs.
